            <ArticleTitle>Outcomes of a care coordinated service model for persons with severe and persistent mental illness: A qualitative study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>40-47</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0020764016678014</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Owing to difficulties faced by individuals with severe and persistent mental illness (SPMI) in accessing multiple services, the Australian Government trialed a care coordinated service model called the Partners in Recovery (PIR) initiative.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 45 stakeholders in Gippsland were asked what difference the initiative had made.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The PIR initiative benefited not only clients and carers but also service providers. It addressed an unmet need in service delivery for individuals with SPMI.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The PIR initiative has filled a gap in delivery of care for individuals with SPMI in Gippsland.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
